Roger Barnett wrote:

> Tenaya

You have by showing how you did this created a whole
potential new and extremely difficult conduct
category, with competition possible, here, the
"Compassionate Pacifist": a perfect pacifist game
with the fewest collateral monster deaths.

A few monsters will fall into traps and such, or to
one another's weapons and wands, and the game will
become heavily a game of managing monsters to keep
them alive and keep them from killing one another,
but still, this one looks like a possible new way to
create bragging rights among the most highly skilled
NetHack players.

What is neat about this is that it creates a real
and essential conflict between the ordinary
Pacifist's "lead the scary monster across the trap
to let it kill itself" and the Compassionate
Pacifist's "lead the scary monster to avoid the
trap, so it won't kill itself".

I expect playing pet free from turn one would be a
frequent strategy.

The brag of course is "I played a pacifist with only
N collateral damage kills", for smallest values of N.

Should such a conduct be required to be genocide
free, too?

